+// The order the unpinned region should sit in: a stable partition of the units
+// by workspace, following `workspace_order`. Returns a permutation of positions
+// into `unit_guids`, not tab indices.
+//
+// A UNIT IS NOT A TAB, and that correction is the whole reason this takes the
+// shape it does. MoveWebContentsAt's own contract says "EnsureGroupContiguity()
+// is called after the move ... though the moved tab's group may change", so
+// partitioning raw tab indices would quietly move tabs into and out of tab
+// groups. The caller therefore clumps the strip into things that must move
+// whole -- a tab group, else a split, else a single tab -- and passes one guid
+// per clump (LOCAL.md 24).
+//
+// The orphan rule is folded in rather than left to the caller: a guid absent
+// from `workspace_order` resolves to the default workspace, exactly as
+// ResolveTabGuid does it and exactly as the render predicate sees it. So
+// `workspace_order` is both the ordering and the known set, and there is no
+// second list to keep in step.
+//
+// STABLE WITHIN A WORKSPACE. Two units of the same workspace never change
+// places. That is what makes this safe to run on a strip the user has arranged
+// by hand: only the interleaving between workspaces goes, and the order they
+// dragged their own tabs into survives.
+//
+// Returns the identity permutation -- reorder nothing -- when `workspace_order`
+// is empty, and again if the partition would not name every unit exactly once.
+// The second is a guard against a caller whose list has lost the default
+// workspace, which ParseWorkspaces() guarantees cannot happen and which would
+// otherwise drop units silently, moving some tabs and abandoning the rest
+// wherever they happened to be.
+std::vector<size_t> ReconciledUnitOrder(
+    const std::vector<std::string>& unit_guids,
+    const std::vector<std::string>& workspace_order);
+
